// ============================================================================
// Forgepoint Auth Service Proto Definitions
// ============================================================================
//
// WHY: The Auth service is the security perimeter for the entire platform.
// Every other service delegates authentication (token validation) and
// authorization (permission checks) here, rather than implementing their own.
// This is the "Centralized Auth" microservices pattern.
//
// WHAT'S HERE:
//   - Core domain messages: User, APIKey, Role, Permission, TokenClaims
//   - AuthService RPCs: login, user management, API keys, RBAC, token validation
//
// PATTERN — Centralized Auth vs Distributed Auth:
//   CENTRALIZED (this approach): One Auth service holds all identity and
//   permission data. Other services call ValidateToken + CheckPermission per
//   request via gRPC interceptors. Simple to reason about, single source of
//   truth, easy to revoke tokens.
//
//   DISTRIBUTED: Each service validates JWTs locally (using the public key)
//   and caches permission decisions. Lower latency, but revocation is hard —
//   a revoked token stays valid until it expires.
//
//   WHY CENTRALIZED FOR FORGEPOINT:
//   - We need real-time revocation (revoking an API key must take effect
//     immediately, not after JWT expiry). Centralized allows us to check
//     a blacklist on every call.
//   - The AuthService can cache ValidateToken results in Redis with a short
//     TTL (~30s), keeping p99 latency acceptable without sacrificing revocability.
//   - Portfolio clarity: one service owns auth, every other service is a client.
//
// REAL-WORLD COMPARISON:
//   - HashiCorp Vault: Centralized secrets + auth, revocation-first design
//   - AWS IAM: Centralized identity; STS tokens are validated via AWS API calls
//   - Istio + JWKS: Distributed JWT validation but no real-time revocation
//   - Keycloak: Centralized IdP, services validate locally (hybrid)
//
// CALL FLOW:
//   Client ──HTTP──► API Gateway ──gRPC──► Auth.Login() → JWT
//   Client ──gRPC──► ModelRegistry (with JWT in metadata)
//                       └──► auth interceptor calls Auth.ValidateToken()
//                       └──► auth interceptor calls Auth.CheckPermission()
//
// VERSIONING: Package path includes v1 following Buf/Google convention.
// Breaking changes require a new forgepoint.auth.v2 package.
// ============================================================================

